+    The five design decisions described in the same work are also relevant: input structuring,
+controlled vocabularies, domain ontology alignment, data-space ontology structuring and RDF
+specification/serialisation [12]. In SafeVoice, these decisions translate into a consistent form/CDM
+contract, stable vocabulary identifiers, agreed predicates and relationships, an explicit topic-record-
+document model, and validated RDF output.
+
+    The VODAN-Africa case demonstrates the use of semantic technologies, controlled vocabularies
+and RDF to support data interoperability in a federated setting [13]. It provides a relevant precedent
+for the direction of SafeVoice, but it does not mean that SafeVoice is already registered or operational
+within an FDP.
